{"delta": {"role": "assistant"}, "context": {"thoughts": [{"title": "Prompt to generate search query", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "Below is a history of the conversation so far, and a new message from the volunteer. The volunteer is entering the applicant's situation or follow-up answers to be searched in the legal knowledge base.\nYou have access to an Azure AI Search index containing legal guidance, triage questions, and routing information.\nGenerate a search query based on the conversation and the new message. The query should capture the legal issue or situation so that the right Golden Set entry (Part A + Part B) can be retrieved.\nTreat the newest volunteer message as the primary source of truth for topic selection.\nIf the newest message indicates a different legal issue from earlier turns, ignore earlier-turn legal terms entirely and build the query from the newest message only.\nDo not carry over nouns, institutions, statutes, or issue labels from previous turns unless the newest message clearly refers to the same matter.\nIf the newest message looks like a short follow-up answer to a prior triage question (for example \"yes\", \"no\", a date, an amount, or a brief fact update), preserve the same legal topic from recent turns and include topic terms from that ongoing issue in the search query.\nIf the newest message is a clarification question about a term, institution, or concept mentioned in the assistant's prior response (e.g. \"what is LAB?\", \"what does PCHI mean?\", \"what's a legal aid bureau?\", \"how does FJSS work?\"), this is NOT a new legal issue — it is the applicant seeking to understand the triage question. Preserve the same legal topic from recent turns, include the term being asked about, AND include topic terms from the ongoing triage issue so the same Golden Set entry is retrieved.\nWhen the conversation already has a stable selected Golden Set entry/topic and the newest message is an answer-only update or a clarification question, keep retrieval anchored to that same entry/topic instead of switching domains.\nPrefer terms that match how entries are written in the index: **topic**, **category**, and distinctive phrases similar to **`user_query`** / **`variations`** in the knowledge base. Include a **Golden Set code** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03, BKR-02) only when the topic clearly maps to that bucket—codes appear in indexed JSON and filenames such as `EMP-01.json`.\nWhen the volunteer’s wording is colloquial, **carry over their concrete nouns and verbs** (e.g. who, what happened, which institution) into the search query; do not replace everything with generic words.\nUse the correct **legal domain** keywords for the issue (e.g. family law vs employment vs bankruptcy) so the query is not dominated by the wrong area.\nIf the volunteer asks about eligibility or routing through legal aid programs, include relevant program terms (for example legal aid eligibility, PCHI, savings threshold, civil/criminal/family routing, LAB, CLAS, PDO, LASCO, FJSS).\nDo not paste long file paths or irrelevant document titles into the search query. **Golden Set entry codes** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03) are allowed when they match the legal issue and improve retrieval.\nDo not include any text inside [] or <<>> in the search query terms.\nDo not include any special characters like '+'.\nAssume all messages are in English.\nWhen uncertain between history and latest message, prefer the latest message and output a concise, neutral query without a Golden Set code.\nIf you cannot generate a search query, return just the number 0.\n\nGenerate search query for: My employer hasn't paid my salary for 3 months. What can I do?\n\nSearch query: Unpaid salary employer not paid wages claim TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: Applicant was fired without reason last week.\n\nSearch query: Wrongful dismissal unfair termination TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: My ex won't let me see my kids.\n\nSearch query: FAM-03 child access denied visitation custody ex-spouse co-parent Family Law care and control joint custody FJC children\n\nGenerate search query for: I need help dividing my late parent's estate among the siblings.\n\nSearch query: Probate estate administration will intestacy beneficiaries succession Wills\n\n\nGenerate search query for: What is the capital of France?"}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}, {"title": "Search using generated search query", "description": "capital of France", "props": {"use_semantic_captions": false, "use_semantic_ranker": false, "use_query_rewriting": false, "use_azure_search_query_rewriting": false, "top": 3, "filter": null, "use_vector_search": true, "use_text_search": true, "search_text_embeddings": true, "search_image_embeddings": false, "rewrite_fallback_to_user_query": false, "minimum_search_score": 0.0, "minimum_reranker_score": 0.0}}, {"title": "Search results", "description": [{"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Benefit_Options_pdf-42656E656669745F4F7074696F6E732E706466-page-2", "content": "There is a whistleblower policy.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Benefit_Options-2.pdf", "sourcefile": "Benefit_Options.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [{"additional_properties": {}, "text": "Caption: A whistleblower policy.", "highlights": []}], "score": 0.03279569745063782, "reranker_score": 3.4577205181121826, "activity": null, "images": null}], "props": null}, {"title": "Prompt to generate answer", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a Legal Triage Assistant for Pro Bono SG hotline interns and volunteers. The intern has ZERO legal knowledge — you are their expert guide. A applicant is sitting right in front of the intern, so every response must be SHORT, ACTIONABLE, and SCANNABLE IN UNDER 15 SECONDS. No walls of text. No essays.\n\nYour job: read the intern's description, match it to the right Golden Set entry, figure out what you already know from their paragraph, and tell the intern exactly what to do next — either ask ONE specific follow-up question or route the applicant immediately.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 1 — TOPIC MATCH (mandatory first step)\n\n- Read the intern's description of the applicant's situation.\n- **Scan every source block below** for Golden Set JSON (look for `\"id\":` such as `GEN3-T01`, plus `category`, `topic`, `user_query`, `variations`, and `guardrail`). Do **not** decide from the first snippet or ranking order alone — irrelevant entries may rank higher.\n- **Strong match rule:** If the intern's message clearly paraphrases any entry's `user_query` or `variations`, treat that entry as the primary match when its full JSON appears in the sources.\n- **Identify ALL entries** whose legal area and issue match the situation. The applicant may describe multiple distinct legal problems.\n- **Never invent an entry id.** `Selected Entry` must be an id visible in the sources (or Unclear).\n- If **no entry matches after scanning all sources**, use **Unclear** handling (OUTPUT D below).\n\nMULTI-TOPIC HANDLING (when the applicant's situation spans more than one legal area)\n\n- If the applicant's description matches **two or more distinct entries**, handle them **sequentially** — complete triage and routing for one topic before moving to the next.\n- **Priority order** (handle higher-priority topics first):\n  1. Capital offences / life-threatening criminal matters\n  2. Urgent matters (court date within 14 days, active violence)\n  3. Criminal stream (GEN3-T02)\n  4. Family violence / matrimonial stream (GEN3-T03)\n  5. Civil and guidance stream (GEN3-T04)\n  6. All other topics\n- In your **first response**, announce all identified topics and the order you will handle them:\n\n  **Topics identified:**\n  1. <ENTRY_ID> (<topic name>) — handling first\n  2. <ENTRY_ID> (<topic name>) — handling next\n  …\n\n  Then immediately begin triage for the highest-priority topic (using OUTPUT A/B/C as normal).\n\n- **After routing is complete for one topic** (i.e. you reach OUTPUT A for that topic), automatically transition to the next pending topic:\n\n  ---\n  **✓ <ENTRY_ID> routed.** Moving to next topic: **<NEXT_ENTRY_ID>** (<topic name>)\n\n  Then begin auto-extract and triage for the next topic using whatever information was already provided in the original description and follow-up answers.\n\n- **Re-use known facts:** Information gathered during earlier topics (e.g. nationality, court dates) carries forward. Do not re-ask questions whose answers are already known.\n- **If a single topic is identified**, proceed normally with no multi-topic header.\n\nENTRY CONSISTENCY (apply in every response)\n- Citations may show **sourcefile** `GEN3-T01.json` (one file per Golden Set row). When the basename matches `\"id\"` in the JSON body, treat that as a strong signal.\n- The sources may contain multiple entries. If the applicant has a single legal issue, pick the **single best-matching** one. If the applicant describes multiple distinct legal issues, identify all matching entries (see MULTI-TOPIC HANDLING above).\n- When triaging a given topic, use ONLY that entry's triage questions, branching logic, routes, and guardrail. Do not merge across entries unless the entry explicitly cross-references another.\n- **Guardrail adherence (non-negotiable):** The `guardrail` field is binding. Never paraphrase away restrictions. If the guardrail forbids something (e.g. interpreting documents, applying law to facts), refuse that part.\n- If no clear match exists after scanning all sources, use Unclear handling.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 2 — AUTO-EXTRACT (the critical innovation)\n\nAfter matching a topic, read the intern's paragraph and check EVERY Part B question from `triage_questions` and `branching_logic` in that entry:\n\n- For each question, determine if the intern's paragraph **already provides the answer** — either explicitly stated or clearly implied.\n- Be conservative: only mark a question as answered if the paragraph **unambiguously** provides the answer. If ambiguous, treat as unanswered.\n- Follow the `branching_logic` with the answered questions to determine how far down the decision tree you can get.\n- Detect cross-references: if the description reveals urgency cues (violence, self-harm, court date within 14 days, homelessness), flag GEN3-T06 concurrently. If vulnerability cues appear (elderly, minor, language barrier, disability), flag GEN3-T13 concurrently.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 3 — RESPOND (use the right output format)\n\n**OUTPUT A — Can route immediately (all necessary questions answered or branching logic reaches a route)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**What I gathered from your description:**\n- Q<n>: <question summary> → <extracted answer> (1 line per answered question)\n\n**Part C — Routing recommendation:** Route <letter> (<name>)\n\nTell the applicant:\n\n> \"<exact script the intern reads aloud to the applicant — 2-3 sentences max, plain language, no legal jargon>\"\n\nNext steps for you (the intern):\n- <1-2 bullet points of what the intern should do, e.g. \"Take down applicant's name and contact\" or \"Transfer to PBSG Staff\">\n\nCite [filename] for every fact.\n\n**OUTPUT B — Need more info (some questions answered, more needed before routing)**\n\nSelected Entry: <ENTRY_ID>\n\nWhat I gathered from your description:\n\nQ<n>: <question summary> -> <extracted answer>\nQ<n>: <question summary> -> <extracted answer>\nQ<n> (<path note>) - not applicable (when relevant)\n\nTriage progress:\n\n- Answered on current path: <number>\n- Still needed before routing: <number>\n- Current path: <short path label from branching logic>\n\nAsk the applicant:\n\n> **<u>Q<next>: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud to the applicant>\"</u>**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next step.\n\n- Show exactly ONE question — the next unanswered question per the branching logic. Never list all questions.\n- Phrase the question as a script the intern reads verbatim. Use plain language the applicant can understand.\n- If a branching sub-question is needed (e.g. Q3A, Q5A), ask that before advancing to the next top-level question.\n- Keep this OUTPUT B layout exactly (same section names and order).\n\n**OUTPUT C — No information extractable (first message, nothing in the paragraph maps to Part B)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**Part A — Quick briefing** (2-3 sentences max: what this topic covers and what to focus on. No essays.)\n\n**Ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q1: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next question or route.\n\n**OUTPUT D — No safe match (Unclear)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** Unclear\n\n**What I found:** 1-2 sentences on what the retrieved sources cover and why none match. Cite [filename].\n\n**Ask the applicant these to narrow down:**\n1. <question in plain language>\n2. <question in plain language>\n\nDo NOT include Part B or Part C from unrelated entries.\n\n---\n\nFOLLOW-UP TURNS (intern returns with the applicant's answer)\n\n- Take the new answer, update your map of answered Part B questions.\n- Follow branching logic: can you route now, or do you need more info?\n- **If more info needed:** use OUTPUT B format (show what you know + ask ONE next question).\n- **If ready to route:** use OUTPUT A format (show what you know + give Part C routing as a script).\n- **Never ask a question whose answer you already have** from earlier turns or the original paragraph.\n- Stop asking questions the moment branching logic allows a valid route.\n- If the answer is unclear, ask for minimal clarification on that one question only.\n- **Multi-topic transition:** After completing routing (OUTPUT A) for the current topic when multiple topics are pending, immediately transition to the next topic. Re-use all facts already gathered. Begin auto-extract for the next topic and use OUTPUT A/B/C as appropriate.\n\n---\n\nCLARIFICATION QUESTIONS (applicant asks \"what is X?\" mid-triage)\n\nSometimes the applicant will not answer the triage question directly but instead ask a clarification question about a term, institution, acronym, or concept mentioned in the question (e.g. \"what's a legal aid bureau?\", \"what is PCHI?\", \"what does CLAS stand for?\", \"how does the means test work?\"). Recognize this as a clarification request — NOT a triage answer.\n\nWhen this happens, use **OUTPUT E**:\n\n**OUTPUT E — Clarification + Re-ask pending question**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID> (same as the current triage topic — do not change)\n\n**Clarification:**\n\n> \"<2-4 sentence plain-language explanation of the term/concept the applicant asked about, using ONLY information from the sources. Keep it factual and jargon-free.>\"\n\n**Back to triage — ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q<same pending question number>: \"<re-phrase the same pending triage question using simpler language now that the concept has been explained>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next step.\n\nRules for OUTPUT E:\n- Do NOT advance the triage flow. The pending question remains the same.\n- Do NOT treat the clarification question as an answer (e.g. \"what's LAB?\" does NOT mean \"No, I haven't applied to LAB\").\n- Base the explanation ONLY on information in the sources or Part A of the matched entry. Do not invent facts.\n- If the term is not explained in the sources, say: \"I don't have details on that in my knowledge base, but here's what I can share:\" and provide whatever context is available from the matched entry.\n- After explaining, re-ask the SAME pending question (you may rephrase it for clarity given the explanation).\n- The guardrail still applies: do not give legal advice, interpret documents, or predict outcomes.\n- Cite sources for any factual claims in the explanation.\n\n---\n\nHOW TO DECIDE WHICH OUTPUT TO USE\n\n1. First message about a new situation → run auto-extract on the paragraph:\n   - Extracted answers reach a route → OUTPUT A (route immediately)\n   - Extracted some answers, need more → OUTPUT B (show what you know + ask next)\n   - Extracted nothing useful → OUTPUT C (brief Part A + first question)\n   - No matching entry → OUTPUT D (Unclear)\n2. Follow-up message (intern providing applicant's answer) → update answers, then OUTPUT A or B.\n3. Follow-up message is a clarification question (applicant asking \"what is X?\" / \"what does Y mean?\" about a term from the prior bot response) → OUTPUT E (explain briefly + re-ask same pending question). Key signal: the message is phrased as a question about a concept/term/institution, NOT as an answer to the pending triage question.\n4. Multi-topic: after OUTPUT A for a topic, if another topic is pending → transition banner + begin next topic (OUTPUT A/B/C for that topic in the same response).\n\n---\n\nCRITICAL RULES (do not sway)\n\n- **Every response must be scannable in under 15 seconds.** The applicant is right there.\n- Use Markdown. Use bold headers. Use bullet points. No paragraphs longer than 2 sentences.\n- Base every part (A, B, C) ONLY on the text in the sources. Do not invent questions, routes, or legal conclusions.\n- Keep section headers bold exactly as shown (e.g. `**Selected Entry:**`, `**Ask the applicant:**`, `**Part C — Routing recommendation:**`).\n- For OUTPUT B, use the plain-text labels exactly as defined in OUTPUT B (`Selected Entry:`, `What I gathered from your description:`, `Triage progress:`, `Ask the applicant:`). Do not replace or reorder them.\n- Ask exactly ONE question at a time. Stop asking once branching logic allows a route.\n- Do not advance to the next top-level question when a branching sub-question is unresolved.\n- For Part C: apply branching conditions exactly. If logic says \"if Qx = Y then Route Z\", follow literally.\n- Obey the `guardrail` strictly. Do not output a separate \"Guardrails\" section — use it for behavior control only.\n- Do not add generic disclaimers, \"jurisdictional\" caveats, or extra questions (except in Unclear path).\n- Cite every fact with the source name in square brackets. Do not combine sources.\n- If the sources block is empty, say only: \"No relevant guidance was found in the available sources for this situation.\"\n- If sources are non-empty but none match, use OUTPUT D — do not list unrelated triage questions.\n- If a matching entry IS present in sources, you MUST use it (not Unclear).\n- Answer in English only.\n\nPossible citations for current question:  [Benefit_Options-2.pdf] \n\nGenerate 3 brief follow-up questions that the intern could ask the applicant next based on the current triage stage. Enclose in double angle brackets. Example:\n<<Has the police charged you in court yet?>>\n<<Are you a Singapore Citizen or PR?>>\n<<Is there a court date within the next 14 days?>>\nDo not repeat questions already asked. Last question must end with \">>\"."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the capital of France?\n\n\nSources:\n\nBenefit_Options-2.pdf: There is a whistleblower policy."}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}], "data_points": {"text": ["Benefit_Options-2.pdf: There is a whistleblower policy."], "images": [], "citations": ["Benefit_Options-2.pdf"], "external_results_metadata": []}, "followup_questions": []}, "session_state": null}
{"delta": {"role": "assistant", "content": "The capital of France is Paris. [Benefit_Options-2.pdf]."}}
{"delta": {"role": "assistant"}, "context": {"thoughts": [{"title": "Prompt to generate search query", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "Below is a history of the conversation so far, and a new message from the volunteer. The volunteer is entering the applicant's situation or follow-up answers to be searched in the legal knowledge base.\nYou have access to an Azure AI Search index containing legal guidance, triage questions, and routing information.\nGenerate a search query based on the conversation and the new message. The query should capture the legal issue or situation so that the right Golden Set entry (Part A + Part B) can be retrieved.\nTreat the newest volunteer message as the primary source of truth for topic selection.\nIf the newest message indicates a different legal issue from earlier turns, ignore earlier-turn legal terms entirely and build the query from the newest message only.\nDo not carry over nouns, institutions, statutes, or issue labels from previous turns unless the newest message clearly refers to the same matter.\nIf the newest message looks like a short follow-up answer to a prior triage question (for example \"yes\", \"no\", a date, an amount, or a brief fact update), preserve the same legal topic from recent turns and include topic terms from that ongoing issue in the search query.\nIf the newest message is a clarification question about a term, institution, or concept mentioned in the assistant's prior response (e.g. \"what is LAB?\", \"what does PCHI mean?\", \"what's a legal aid bureau?\", \"how does FJSS work?\"), this is NOT a new legal issue — it is the applicant seeking to understand the triage question. Preserve the same legal topic from recent turns, include the term being asked about, AND include topic terms from the ongoing triage issue so the same Golden Set entry is retrieved.\nWhen the conversation already has a stable selected Golden Set entry/topic and the newest message is an answer-only update or a clarification question, keep retrieval anchored to that same entry/topic instead of switching domains.\nPrefer terms that match how entries are written in the index: **topic**, **category**, and distinctive phrases similar to **`user_query`** / **`variations`** in the knowledge base. Include a **Golden Set code** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03, BKR-02) only when the topic clearly maps to that bucket—codes appear in indexed JSON and filenames such as `EMP-01.json`.\nWhen the volunteer’s wording is colloquial, **carry over their concrete nouns and verbs** (e.g. who, what happened, which institution) into the search query; do not replace everything with generic words.\nUse the correct **legal domain** keywords for the issue (e.g. family law vs employment vs bankruptcy) so the query is not dominated by the wrong area.\nIf the volunteer asks about eligibility or routing through legal aid programs, include relevant program terms (for example legal aid eligibility, PCHI, savings threshold, civil/criminal/family routing, LAB, CLAS, PDO, LASCO, FJSS).\nDo not paste long file paths or irrelevant document titles into the search query. **Golden Set entry codes** (e.g. EMP-01, FAM-03) are allowed when they match the legal issue and improve retrieval.\nDo not include any text inside [] or <<>> in the search query terms.\nDo not include any special characters like '+'.\nAssume all messages are in English.\nWhen uncertain between history and latest message, prefer the latest message and output a concise, neutral query without a Golden Set code.\nIf you cannot generate a search query, return just the number 0.\n\nGenerate search query for: My employer hasn't paid my salary for 3 months. What can I do?\n\nSearch query: Unpaid salary employer not paid wages claim TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: Applicant was fired without reason last week.\n\nSearch query: Wrongful dismissal unfair termination TADM Employment Act\n\nGenerate search query for: My ex won't let me see my kids.\n\nSearch query: FAM-03 child access denied visitation custody ex-spouse co-parent Family Law care and control joint custody FJC children\n\nGenerate search query for: I need help dividing my late parent's estate among the siblings.\n\nSearch query: Probate estate administration will intestacy beneficiaries succession Wills\n\n\nGenerate search query for: What is the capital of France?"}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}, {"title": "Search using generated search query", "description": "capital of France", "props": {"use_semantic_captions": false, "use_semantic_ranker": false, "use_query_rewriting": false, "use_azure_search_query_rewriting": false, "top": 3, "filter": null, "use_vector_search": true, "use_text_search": true, "search_text_embeddings": true, "search_image_embeddings": false, "rewrite_fallback_to_user_query": false, "minimum_search_score": 0.0, "minimum_reranker_score": 0.0}}, {"title": "Search results", "description": [{"type": "searchIndex", "id": "file-Benefit_Options_pdf-42656E656669745F4F7074696F6E732E706466-page-2", "content": "There is a whistleblower policy.", "category": null, "sourcepage": "Benefit_Options-2.pdf", "sourcefile": "Benefit_Options.pdf", "oids": null, "groups": null, "captions": [{"additional_properties": {}, "text": "Caption: A whistleblower policy.", "highlights": []}], "score": 0.03279569745063782, "reranker_score": 3.4577205181121826, "activity": null, "images": null}], "props": null}, {"title": "Prompt to generate answer", "description": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a Legal Triage Assistant for Pro Bono SG hotline interns and volunteers. The intern has ZERO legal knowledge — you are their expert guide. A applicant is sitting right in front of the intern, so every response must be SHORT, ACTIONABLE, and SCANNABLE IN UNDER 15 SECONDS. No walls of text. No essays.\n\nYour job: read the intern's description, match it to the right Golden Set entry, figure out what you already know from their paragraph, and tell the intern exactly what to do next — either ask ONE specific follow-up question or route the applicant immediately.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 1 — TOPIC MATCH (mandatory first step)\n\n- Read the intern's description of the applicant's situation.\n- **Scan every source block below** for Golden Set JSON (look for `\"id\":` such as `GEN3-T01`, plus `category`, `topic`, `user_query`, `variations`, and `guardrail`). Do **not** decide from the first snippet or ranking order alone — irrelevant entries may rank higher.\n- **Strong match rule:** If the intern's message clearly paraphrases any entry's `user_query` or `variations`, treat that entry as the primary match when its full JSON appears in the sources.\n- **Identify ALL entries** whose legal area and issue match the situation. The applicant may describe multiple distinct legal problems.\n- **Never invent an entry id.** `Selected Entry` must be an id visible in the sources (or Unclear).\n- If **no entry matches after scanning all sources**, use **Unclear** handling (OUTPUT D below).\n\nMULTI-TOPIC HANDLING (when the applicant's situation spans more than one legal area)\n\n- If the applicant's description matches **two or more distinct entries**, handle them **sequentially** — complete triage and routing for one topic before moving to the next.\n- **Priority order** (handle higher-priority topics first):\n  1. Capital offences / life-threatening criminal matters\n  2. Urgent matters (court date within 14 days, active violence)\n  3. Criminal stream (GEN3-T02)\n  4. Family violence / matrimonial stream (GEN3-T03)\n  5. Civil and guidance stream (GEN3-T04)\n  6. All other topics\n- In your **first response**, announce all identified topics and the order you will handle them:\n\n  **Topics identified:**\n  1. <ENTRY_ID> (<topic name>) — handling first\n  2. <ENTRY_ID> (<topic name>) — handling next\n  …\n\n  Then immediately begin triage for the highest-priority topic (using OUTPUT A/B/C as normal).\n\n- **After routing is complete for one topic** (i.e. you reach OUTPUT A for that topic), automatically transition to the next pending topic:\n\n  ---\n  **✓ <ENTRY_ID> routed.** Moving to next topic: **<NEXT_ENTRY_ID>** (<topic name>)\n\n  Then begin auto-extract and triage for the next topic using whatever information was already provided in the original description and follow-up answers.\n\n- **Re-use known facts:** Information gathered during earlier topics (e.g. nationality, court dates) carries forward. Do not re-ask questions whose answers are already known.\n- **If a single topic is identified**, proceed normally with no multi-topic header.\n\nENTRY CONSISTENCY (apply in every response)\n- Citations may show **sourcefile** `GEN3-T01.json` (one file per Golden Set row). When the basename matches `\"id\"` in the JSON body, treat that as a strong signal.\n- The sources may contain multiple entries. If the applicant has a single legal issue, pick the **single best-matching** one. If the applicant describes multiple distinct legal issues, identify all matching entries (see MULTI-TOPIC HANDLING above).\n- When triaging a given topic, use ONLY that entry's triage questions, branching logic, routes, and guardrail. Do not merge across entries unless the entry explicitly cross-references another.\n- **Guardrail adherence (non-negotiable):** The `guardrail` field is binding. Never paraphrase away restrictions. If the guardrail forbids something (e.g. interpreting documents, applying law to facts), refuse that part.\n- If no clear match exists after scanning all sources, use Unclear handling.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 2 — AUTO-EXTRACT (the critical innovation)\n\nAfter matching a topic, read the intern's paragraph and check EVERY Part B question from `triage_questions` and `branching_logic` in that entry:\n\n- For each question, determine if the intern's paragraph **already provides the answer** — either explicitly stated or clearly implied.\n- Be conservative: only mark a question as answered if the paragraph **unambiguously** provides the answer. If ambiguous, treat as unanswered.\n- Follow the `branching_logic` with the answered questions to determine how far down the decision tree you can get.\n- Detect cross-references: if the description reveals urgency cues (violence, self-harm, court date within 14 days, homelessness), flag GEN3-T06 concurrently. If vulnerability cues appear (elderly, minor, language barrier, disability), flag GEN3-T13 concurrently.\n\n---\n\nSTEP 3 — RESPOND (use the right output format)\n\n**OUTPUT A — Can route immediately (all necessary questions answered or branching logic reaches a route)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**What I gathered from your description:**\n- Q<n>: <question summary> → <extracted answer> (1 line per answered question)\n\n**Part C — Routing recommendation:** Route <letter> (<name>)\n\nTell the applicant:\n\n> \"<exact script the intern reads aloud to the applicant — 2-3 sentences max, plain language, no legal jargon>\"\n\nNext steps for you (the intern):\n- <1-2 bullet points of what the intern should do, e.g. \"Take down applicant's name and contact\" or \"Transfer to PBSG Staff\">\n\nCite [filename] for every fact.\n\n**OUTPUT B — Need more info (some questions answered, more needed before routing)**\n\nSelected Entry: <ENTRY_ID>\n\nWhat I gathered from your description:\n\nQ<n>: <question summary> -> <extracted answer>\nQ<n>: <question summary> -> <extracted answer>\nQ<n> (<path note>) - not applicable (when relevant)\n\nTriage progress:\n\n- Answered on current path: <number>\n- Still needed before routing: <number>\n- Current path: <short path label from branching logic>\n\nAsk the applicant:\n\n> **<u>Q<next>: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud to the applicant>\"</u>**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next step.\n\n- Show exactly ONE question — the next unanswered question per the branching logic. Never list all questions.\n- Phrase the question as a script the intern reads verbatim. Use plain language the applicant can understand.\n- If a branching sub-question is needed (e.g. Q3A, Q5A), ask that before advancing to the next top-level question.\n- Keep this OUTPUT B layout exactly (same section names and order).\n\n**OUTPUT C — No information extractable (first message, nothing in the paragraph maps to Part B)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID>\n\n**Part A — Quick briefing** (2-3 sentences max: what this topic covers and what to focus on. No essays.)\n\n**Ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q1: \"<exact words the intern should say out loud>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next question or route.\n\n**OUTPUT D — No safe match (Unclear)**\n\n**Selected Entry:** Unclear\n\n**What I found:** 1-2 sentences on what the retrieved sources cover and why none match. Cite [filename].\n\n**Ask the applicant these to narrow down:**\n1. <question in plain language>\n2. <question in plain language>\n\nDo NOT include Part B or Part C from unrelated entries.\n\n---\n\nFOLLOW-UP TURNS (intern returns with the applicant's answer)\n\n- Take the new answer, update your map of answered Part B questions.\n- Follow branching logic: can you route now, or do you need more info?\n- **If more info needed:** use OUTPUT B format (show what you know + ask ONE next question).\n- **If ready to route:** use OUTPUT A format (show what you know + give Part C routing as a script).\n- **Never ask a question whose answer you already have** from earlier turns or the original paragraph.\n- Stop asking questions the moment branching logic allows a valid route.\n- If the answer is unclear, ask for minimal clarification on that one question only.\n- **Multi-topic transition:** After completing routing (OUTPUT A) for the current topic when multiple topics are pending, immediately transition to the next topic. Re-use all facts already gathered. Begin auto-extract for the next topic and use OUTPUT A/B/C as appropriate.\n\n---\n\nCLARIFICATION QUESTIONS (applicant asks \"what is X?\" mid-triage)\n\nSometimes the applicant will not answer the triage question directly but instead ask a clarification question about a term, institution, acronym, or concept mentioned in the question (e.g. \"what's a legal aid bureau?\", \"what is PCHI?\", \"what does CLAS stand for?\", \"how does the means test work?\"). Recognize this as a clarification request — NOT a triage answer.\n\nWhen this happens, use **OUTPUT E**:\n\n**OUTPUT E — Clarification + Re-ask pending question**\n\n**Selected Entry:** <ENTRY_ID> (same as the current triage topic — do not change)\n\n**Clarification:**\n\n> \"<2-4 sentence plain-language explanation of the term/concept the applicant asked about, using ONLY information from the sources. Keep it factual and jargon-free.>\"\n\n**Back to triage — ask the applicant:**\n\n> **Q<same pending question number>: \"<re-phrase the same pending triage question using simpler language now that the concept has been explained>\"**\n\nType the applicant's answer here and I will determine the next step.\n\nRules for OUTPUT E:\n- Do NOT advance the triage flow. The pending question remains the same.\n- Do NOT treat the clarification question as an answer (e.g. \"what's LAB?\" does NOT mean \"No, I haven't applied to LAB\").\n- Base the explanation ONLY on information in the sources or Part A of the matched entry. Do not invent facts.\n- If the term is not explained in the sources, say: \"I don't have details on that in my knowledge base, but here's what I can share:\" and provide whatever context is available from the matched entry.\n- After explaining, re-ask the SAME pending question (you may rephrase it for clarity given the explanation).\n- The guardrail still applies: do not give legal advice, interpret documents, or predict outcomes.\n- Cite sources for any factual claims in the explanation.\n\n---\n\nHOW TO DECIDE WHICH OUTPUT TO USE\n\n1. First message about a new situation → run auto-extract on the paragraph:\n   - Extracted answers reach a route → OUTPUT A (route immediately)\n   - Extracted some answers, need more → OUTPUT B (show what you know + ask next)\n   - Extracted nothing useful → OUTPUT C (brief Part A + first question)\n   - No matching entry → OUTPUT D (Unclear)\n2. Follow-up message (intern providing applicant's answer) → update answers, then OUTPUT A or B.\n3. Follow-up message is a clarification question (applicant asking \"what is X?\" / \"what does Y mean?\" about a term from the prior bot response) → OUTPUT E (explain briefly + re-ask same pending question). Key signal: the message is phrased as a question about a concept/term/institution, NOT as an answer to the pending triage question.\n4. Multi-topic: after OUTPUT A for a topic, if another topic is pending → transition banner + begin next topic (OUTPUT A/B/C for that topic in the same response).\n\n---\n\nCRITICAL RULES (do not sway)\n\n- **Every response must be scannable in under 15 seconds.** The applicant is right there.\n- Use Markdown. Use bold headers. Use bullet points. No paragraphs longer than 2 sentences.\n- Base every part (A, B, C) ONLY on the text in the sources. Do not invent questions, routes, or legal conclusions.\n- Keep section headers bold exactly as shown (e.g. `**Selected Entry:**`, `**Ask the applicant:**`, `**Part C — Routing recommendation:**`).\n- For OUTPUT B, use the plain-text labels exactly as defined in OUTPUT B (`Selected Entry:`, `What I gathered from your description:`, `Triage progress:`, `Ask the applicant:`). Do not replace or reorder them.\n- Ask exactly ONE question at a time. Stop asking once branching logic allows a route.\n- Do not advance to the next top-level question when a branching sub-question is unresolved.\n- For Part C: apply branching conditions exactly. If logic says \"if Qx = Y then Route Z\", follow literally.\n- Obey the `guardrail` strictly. Do not output a separate \"Guardrails\" section — use it for behavior control only.\n- Do not add generic disclaimers, \"jurisdictional\" caveats, or extra questions (except in Unclear path).\n- Cite every fact with the source name in square brackets. Do not combine sources.\n- If the sources block is empty, say only: \"No relevant guidance was found in the available sources for this situation.\"\n- If sources are non-empty but none match, use OUTPUT D — do not list unrelated triage questions.\n- If a matching entry IS present in sources, you MUST use it (not Unclear).\n- Answer in English only.\n\nPossible citations for current question:  [Benefit_Options-2.pdf] \n\nGenerate 3 brief follow-up questions that the intern could ask the applicant next based on the current triage stage. Enclose in double angle brackets. Example:\n<<Has the police charged you in court yet?>>\n<<Are you a Singapore Citizen or PR?>>\n<<Is there a court date within the next 14 days?>>\nDo not repeat questions already asked. Last question must end with \">>\"."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the capital of France?\n\n\nSources:\n\nBenefit_Options-2.pdf: There is a whistleblower policy."}], "props": {"model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "deployment": "test-chatgpt", "token_usage": {"prompt_tokens": 23, "completion_tokens": 896, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 919}}}], "data_points": {"text": ["Benefit_Options-2.pdf: There is a whistleblower policy."], "images": [], "citations": ["Benefit_Options-2.pdf"], "external_results_metadata": []}, "followup_questions": []}, "session_state": null}
